}
</style>
<small>shape: (3, 2)</small><table border="1" class="dataframe"><thead><tr><th>Represent</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>str</td><td>i64</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&quot;Area code&quot;</td><td>563</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Exchange code&quot;</td><td>384</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Line number&quot;</td><td>9905</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
```
:::
:::


## Collapse rows into one

We can construct the full phone number from the three rows using the Polars expression `str.join` like this:

::: {#3790ac31 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
(df
 .select((pl.col('Value').str.join('-')).alias('Phone_No'))
 )
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```{=html}
<div><style>
.dataframe > thead > tr,
.dataframe > tbody > tr {
  text-align: right;
  white-space: pre-wrap;
}
</style>
<small>shape: (1, 1)</small><table border="1" class="dataframe"><thead><tr><th>Phone_No</th></tr><tr><td>str</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&quot;563-384-9905&quot;</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
```
:::
:::


::: {.callout-note}
This is different from the `implode` function, which returns a list of values instead of a string.
:::

::: {#a352e95d .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
(df
 .select(pl.col('Value').implode().alias('Phone_No'))
 )
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}
```{=html}
<div><style>
.dataframe > thead > tr,
.dataframe > tbody > tr {
  text-align: right;
  white-space: pre-wrap;
}
</style>
<small>shape: (1, 1)</small><table border="1" class="dataframe"><thead><tr><th>Phone_No</th></tr><tr><td>list[i64]</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>[563, 384, 9905]</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
```
